/**
@page interface_design Interface design and usage

@section design_state Why do some constructors need a state object?

Even the most trivial string buffer implementation needs state, typically
a pointer to a character array; the state for a string buffer
of type @ref strb_t combines a character array, a recorded length, an error
indicator, an editing position and an editing mode.  The choice is who
supplies storage for that state.

With @ref strb_use, the caller supplies both a character array and a
@ref strbstate_t object. No allocation is needed, so initialisation cannot fail.
Both objects must remain alive for as long as the buffer is used.  Because no
storage is allocated by the library, this constructor can be provided even by
freestanding implementations that do not provide @c malloc.

With @ref strb_alloc, the library supplies storage for both the state and the
characters. The caller manages the buffer through the returned @ref strb_t
pointer and releases it with @ref strb_free. Creation can fail if storage is
unavailable.

The output and editing functions use the same interface in either case.
A text producer therefore need not know how its caller created the buffer.

@section design_model Output controlled by position and mode

A string buffer of type @ref strb_t has a current editing position and
editing mode. The @ref strb_putc, @ref strb_nputc, @ref strb_puts, @ref strb_nputs,
@ref strb_putf and @ref strb_vputf functions
write at that position and advance it. Position and mode determine how the
output affects existing text:

- @ref strb_insert makes room for the output by moving the following text.
- @ref strb_overwrite replaces existing characters and extends the buffer when
  the output reaches beyond its end. It does not remove any remaining suffix.

The initial mode is insertion and the initial position is the end of the
string. Consequently, successive output calls naturally append. Use
@ref strb_seek and @ref strb_setmode when a different editing operation is wanted.
Positions and lengths are measured in bytes; callers editing multibyte text
must choose positions at suitable character boundaries.

Positions saved with @ref strb_tell are numeric offsets rather than borrowed
pointers, so they remain usable if the character array is relocated during
growth. Inserting or deleting text before a saved position can still change
which character it identifies.

The @ref strb_cpy, @ref strb_ncpy, @ref strb_printf and @ref strb_vprintf
functions replace the whole string, regardless of the current position and
mode. On success, they set the position to the end of the replacement text.
Their individual contracts describe these whole-string operations.

@section design_missing Why are there no append, prepend or insert functions?

The output functions let a helper produce text without deciding where that
text belongs. Its caller can arrange for the same helper to append, prepend,
insert or overwrite. For example, this producer changes neither mode nor
position explicitly and can be used in any of those contexts. It writes a
slash followed by a URI path segment. The caller supplies a segment already
encoded for use in a URI; the fixed ASCII segments below need no percent-encoding:

@snippet design.c producer

If the helper always sought the end, or used an append-only operation, callers
could no longer use it to generate text elsewhere. A separate family for each
editing operation would also duplicate the character, repeated-character,
string, bounded-string and formatted-output interfaces.

A separate append function would also need a rule for the position after the
call. If it preserved the old position, subsequent ordinary output could write
in the middle of the string, despite the preceding append. If it left the
position at the new end, it would interrupt editing at the old position. For
example, appending a diagnostic while inserting text elsewhere would change
where the next insertion occurs. Seeking the end explicitly makes that change
visible to the caller; saving and restoring the position is an explicit choice
when the earlier editing is to continue.

Prepending is insertion at position zero. Insertion at another position uses
the same output functions. The short compositions below make both choices
explicit. They also show what state subsequent output will use.

A hypothetical prepend convenience function would have to decide whether to
preserve the previous mode and position. Preserving the numeric position and
preserving its relationship to the original text are different choices after
characters have been inserted before it. The existing primitives leave these
choices with the caller. A caller that temporarily changes editing state can
save it with @ref strb_getmode and @ref strb_tell, then restore the state it
needs. In configurations that enable @ref strb_unputc, setting position or
mode also discards the character available to that operation.

There is no inherent efficiency advantage to naming an operation `prepend`.
The implementation already knows when insertion is happening at position zero.
Allocation strategies, including keeping spare capacity, are independent of
how the operation is named.

@section design_deletion Why is there no delete function?

The same approach of composing position and mode with one operation applies
to deletion. @ref strb_delto uses the current position and a target position
as the endpoints of a range. The target can be before or after the current
position, so separate forward-deletion and backward-deletion functions are
unnecessary. In insertion mode, it removes that range, closes the gap, and
leaves the position at the lower endpoint.

@ref strb_delto returns no value and does not report failure. A target beyond the
end is allowed; the deletion is limited to existing characters.

Deleting an arbitrary range can require a mode change and a seek as well as
@ref strb_delto. For this less common operation, the interface favours
composition over additional convenience functions.

A helper can save @ref strb_tell before generating text, then pass that saved
position to @ref strb_delto without counting how many characters were produced.
Mode remains the caller's choice: insertion mode removes the inserted text;
overwrite mode rewinds the position without moving the following text or
restoring overwritten characters.

The mode-dependent behaviour also supports correcting a short direct write.
Insertion creates space that must be removed if unused. In overwrite mode,
the unused part of the prepared range can still contain existing characters.
Short-write correction must leave those characters in place.
See @ref design_short_write for the correction and its limits.

In insertion mode, a target of @c SIZE_MAX deletes to the end and a target of
zero deletes back to the beginning. The "Deletion of characters" section in
<a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3306.pdf#page=60">N3306</a>
explains the choice of an endpoint-based interface in more detail.

@section design_examples Editing a URI

The following snippets build a relative URI, add a scheme and authority, then
edit its path. They are successive parts of one example in @c examples/design.c.
The caller supplies both the character array and the buffer state:

@snippet design.c external

Passing the array size once associates it with the buffer object. Later output
calls need no destination-size argument. The buffer cannot grow beyond that
array. The examples check individual return values for immediate failure, or
use @ref strb_error after a sequence of calls.

@subsection design_sequential Ordinary sequential output

Two calls to the same producer build the path @c /manual/guide.html without
repositioning:

@snippet design.c sequential

@subsection design_append Append after other editing

If the position may have moved, explicitly seek the end before appending.
At the end, insertion and overwrite mode both extend the buffer, so appending
needs no mode change. Here, the appended text identifies a fragment:

@snippet design.c append

@subsection design_prepend Prepend a prefix

Select insertion mode as well as position zero; overwrite mode would replace
characters at the start. This example leaves insertion mode selected and the
position after the scheme and authority, immediately before the existing path:

@snippet design.c prepend

The example records the path position with @ref strb_tell for subsequent
editing, so those operations do not need to know the prefix length.

@subsection design_insert Insert generated text

The same producer used for sequential output inserts a @c docs segment before
the existing path. The preceding step leaves the buffer in insertion mode
and at the start of the path, ready for the producer:

@snippet design.c insert

@subsection design_overwrite Overwrite existing text

Changing the mode lets that producer overwrite instead. Here, @c help replaces
@c docs without changing the segment length. In general, a shorter replacement
would leave part of the previous segment in place, while a longer one would
overwrite following text:

@snippet design.c overwrite

@subsection design_delete Delete a range

The current position is at the end of the range.
Select insertion mode and delete to the start of the range (keeping the
scheme and authority intact).
Here, the @c /help segment is removed. The path position was recorded while
generating and editing the URI, so deletion does not depend on knowing the
segment length in advance.
The position remains at the start of the range, ready for further output there:

@snippet design.c delete

@section design_reuse One producer, different consumers

The next example adapts the "List of fruit" example in
<a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3306.pdf">WG14 N3306</a>,
page 47. The producer knows neither who supplies the storage nor how its output
will be used. Every index supplied to the producer must be less than
four. The separator depends on the number of entries already generated in this
call, so inserting the list into an existing string does not introduce a
leading comma:

@snippet design.c fruit_producer

@subsection design_fruit_sentence Inserting into a caller-supplied array

This consumer supplies both storage and buffer state. It writes the prefix and
records the position with @ref strb_tell, then adds the full stop. Seeking back to the
recorded position lets the producer insert its list before the punctuation. The
consumer passes the completed array directly to @c puts:

@snippet design.c fruit_sentence

The output is:
@verbatim
Fruit: apple,banana,orange.
@endverbatim

@subsection design_fruit_rows Producing successive results in allocated storage

This consumer obtains an allocated buffer and uses the same producer to build
two standalone lists. It prints each result before resetting the buffer for the
next one, retaining the allocated storage. Replacement by @ref strb_cpy replaces
the whole string and sets the position to the end of the new text; the producer
then writes at that position. The consumer also releases the buffer when done:

@snippet design.c fruit_caller

The output is:
@verbatim
apple,banana,orange
lime,orange,apple
@endverbatim

@section design_errors Deferred errors and operation boundaries

The error indicator is sticky: a successful call does not clear an earlier
failure. A producer can make several output calls and let its caller check
@ref strb_error once before consuming the completed result. Use
@ref strb_clearerr explicitly when recovering from an error.

For a single operation, checking its return value is often more convenient.
For example, @ref strb_puts and @ref strb_putf return zero on success and EOF
on failure. The @c put_path_segment producer in @ref design_missing returns the result
of @ref strb_putf directly, so its caller can check it immediately, as in
@ref design_overwrite, or defer checking until after several calls, as in
@ref design_sequential and @ref design_insert.
A successful return describes that operation; it does not clear or report an
earlier failure recorded by @ref strb_error.

For example, if @ref strb_seek fails, the position is unchanged. A subsequent
@ref strb_puts can succeed at that old position while the error indicator remains
set. Checking only the return value of that output call would miss the failed
seek. Either check each operation that can fail, or check @ref strb_error after
the sequence before consuming its result. Deferring the check does not prevent
successful operations after a failure from changing the buffer.

Seeking to an existing position, including the end obtained from @ref strb_len,
does not require allocation. General seeking also permits positions beyond the
current length, but can fail if the requested position is unsupported. Storage
for output at such a position is obtained when output is attempted, not by the
seek itself.

A bulk output operation that fails because storage cannot be obtained leaves
its previous contents, mode and position intact. This guarantee applies to the
individual operation. A helper making several output calls is not a transaction:
if a later call fails, earlier successful calls have already modified the buffer.

@section design_strings Using existing string functions

The interface produces ordinary null-terminated character arrays. Read-only
functions such as @c strlen and @c strchr can use @ref strb_cptr; consumers such
as @c puts can use the resulting string directly, as in the examples above.

Mutable access through @ref strb_ptr also permits operations such as BSD
@c strsep, which replaces delimiters with null characters without moving text.
These writes do not update the buffer's recorded length or editing position.
After splitting, @ref strb_len still describes the stored character sequence,
while @c strlen describes only its first null-terminated string. Subsequent
output uses the recorded position and mode, not the end of that first string.
Token pointers must not be retained across buffer operations that invalidate
the borrowed pointer; finish consuming them before reusing or growing the buffer.

@section design_aliasing Can a string buffer supply its own input?

The source passed to @ref strb_puts, @ref strb_nputs, @ref strb_cpy or
@ref strb_ncpy must not be derived from the result of calling @ref strb_cptr or
@ref strb_ptr on the same buffer. Self-copying is also unsupported when the
source accesses the same storage through the original caller-supplied array.
This applies to appending as well as inserting or replacing text. Preparing
space can move the source characters or relocate
their storage before they are read. Overlapping copies can also violate the
copy operation's requirements. Using @c memmove alone would not solve these
problems.

The same restriction applies to the format string and strings read through
format arguments by @ref strb_putf, @ref strb_vputf, @ref strb_printf and
@ref strb_vprintf. Make an independent copy before using existing buffer
contents as input. These functions do not promise to detect such aliasing or
report it through @ref strb_error.

@section design_efficiency Space preparation is the common operation

@subsection design_prepare Preparing space for output

The common basis of positioned output is @ref strb_write. Each output function
first determines its character count, prepares that much space, and fills it.
Preparation applies the position and editing mode, grows storage if permitted,
moves the suffix for insertion, and advances the position. A bulk insertion can
therefore move the suffix once rather than once per character. The tests compare
output functions with reference functions that use @ref strb_write and fill the
space independently.

A count of zero still prepares space. At a position beyond the old end it
extends the recorded length to that position and fills the gap with nulls.
An empty @ref strb_puts is therefore not necessarily a no-op.

@subsection design_short_write Producing fewer characters than prepared

@ref strb_write advances the position and updates the length for the requested
count before the caller writes anything. If a writer produces fewer characters,
save the starting position before preparation and pass that position plus the
actual count to @ref strb_delto afterwards, keeping the same editing mode.

For example, this C11 producer writes the multibyte representation of a non-null
character using the caller's position and editing mode. @c c32rtomb returns the
number of bytes written, or @c (size_t)-1 on conversion failure:

@snippet design.c short_write

The conversion uses the current locale and starts in the initial conversion
state. No @ref strb_restore is needed because a non-null character's conversion
adds no terminator. Preparation still requires room for @c MB_LEN_MAX bytes,
even if the actual output would be shorter. Conversion failure is reported by
the helper; it does not set the buffer's error indicator. The caller must check
this helper's return value: checking only @ref strb_error after a sequence of
calls would miss a conversion failure.

The call to @ref strb_delto sets the position to the end of the actual output
in both modes. Its effect on the contents and length depends on the mode:

- In insertion mode, it removes the unused prepared space, reduces the length,
  and moves the suffix back to follow the actual output. A null terminator
  written after that output is in the removed space when the actual count is
  less than the prepared count.
- In overwrite mode, it leaves the characters and length unchanged. This
  preserves the original suffix when the writer emits only the actual output
  characters. If preparation extended the buffer, the excess extension remains.

A writer that appends a null terminator can overwrite the first character of
the suffix in overwrite mode. @ref strb_write saves the character at the
prepared end, not at an earlier actual end, so @ref strb_restore cannot repair
that short write. Determine the exact count before preparation, or generate into separate
storage and copy the result with @ref strb_nputs.

The "Direct insertion" discussion in
<a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3306.pdf#page=65">N3306</a>
explains why @ref strb_restore cannot always restore the first character of the
suffix in overwrite mode. Before the number of characters produced is known,
@ref strb_write would have to save every potentially overwritten character.
This would require extra storage, including for caller-supplied arrays.

@subsection design_restore Direct writing, splitting and restoration

A successful @ref strb_write saves the character at the new position so that
@ref strb_restore can put it back later. This happens even with a count of zero;
each call replaces the previously saved character. If a direct writer puts its
terminating null over that character, @ref strb_restore repairs it. It restores
only that character; it does not undo the output or change the position or
recorded length.

@ref strb_split is equivalent to calling @ref strb_write with a count of zero
and storing a null character at the returned address. It temporarily terminates
the string at the current position without moving the following text or
advancing the position. Like any zero-count preparation, it extends the recorded
length if the position is beyond the old end; otherwise the recorded length is
unchanged. Before an intervening operation discards or replaces the saved
character, @ref strb_restore can replace that null with the saved character,
making the following text accessible as part of the string again.

See those functions' contracts before using the direct-write interface.
Borrowed pointers into the buffer should be obtained again after operations
that may reallocate storage.

The @ref strb_putc, @ref strb_nputc, @ref strb_puts, @ref strb_nputs,
@ref strb_putf and @ref strb_vputf functions preserve the character immediately
following their output. After a successful call to any of these functions,
@ref strb_restore leaves the buffer contents unchanged, including when the
successful call produced no characters.

@section design_unputc Single-character undo

@ref strb_unputc is separate from space preparation and boundary repair:
it steps back one position, removing
an inserted character or restoring an overwritten character. Nonempty output
makes its last character available to this operation; empty output preserves
any previously available character. It does not undo a whole output call.

@section design_further Further reading

<a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3306.pdf">N3306</a>
discusses insertion, positioning and editing on pages 42-44 and gives
additional usage examples on pages 47-51. The function documentation in
@ref strb.h specifies the requirements and guarantees for each function.
*/
